<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import CopyEmail from './CopyEmail.abide'
import Invoice from './Invoice.abide'
import RefreshInvoice from './RefreshInvoice.abide'
import Unpaid from './Unpaid.abide'
import UnreachableInvoice from './UnreachableInvoice.abide'
import copyEmailSource from './CopyEmail.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoiceSource from './Invoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import refreshInvoiceSource from './RefreshInvoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import unpaidSource from './Unpaid.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import unreachableInvoiceSource from './UnreachableInvoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The probes">
        <p>A probe answers about the load without reading the value. <strong>A probe never throws
        and never starts work</strong>, so it is safe in a branch that runs before anything has
        arrived.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>True when</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>busy()</code></td><td>work is in flight — a first load, a reload, or a stream that has not closed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ready()</code></td><td>there is a value to serve</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>error()</code></td><td>hands back the standing refusal — what the last write or production was refused with</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Two booleans, four states, and the states an interface actually shows are the
        combinations:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The state</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>loading, nothing to show</td><td><code>busy() &amp;&amp; !ready()</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>reloading over a value on screen</td><td><code>busy() &amp;&amp; ready()</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>finished, whichever way</td><td><code>!busy()</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>nothing landed and nothing in flight</td><td>neither</td></tr>
                <tr><td>marked stale by <code>invalidate</code>, once a read or a probe runs the producer</td><td><code>busy() &amp;&amp; !ready()</code> (7.8, 7.17)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>busy</code> and <code>ready</code> also have <strong>free</strong> forms that take
        a selection. Every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>Reactive</code> reference</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A read starts the load and leaves a hole">
        <Example
            id="probe-hole"
            title="A read starts the load and leaves a hole"
            summary="An invoice with no loading branch anywhere in it. Reload the page and watch the three fields fill."
            files={{
                'Invoice.abide': invoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <Invoice />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 read, 0 loading branches</em> — against a loading flag, an error branch and a value
        kept in step by hand.</p>

        <p>Reading is the whole subscription — there is no mount hook to put the load in and no
        <code>load()</code> to call. That page has no loading branch in it: the read starts the
        load, the document goes out with a hole where <code>invoice.number</code> was, and the hole
        fills when the row lands. What a read gives back depends only on where the value has got
        to:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The value is</th><th>A read gives you</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>landed</td><td>the value</td></tr>
                <tr><td>busy with nothing to serve — its own load, or one it derives from</td><td>it throws <code>NotLanded</code>, and a sink opens where it was read</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a producer failed with nothing landed</td><td>it throws, to the nearest <code>{'{#try}'}</code> or to <code>error.abide</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>holding a refusal over a value</td><td>the last accepted value; <code>error()</code> carries the refusal</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A read of a value with nothing landed throws <code>NotLanded</code> (2.9), and inside a
        tracked body that throw <strong>aborts the body</strong> rather than letting it branch on an
        absence (2.12). The body runs again when the value lands (2.13). So
        <code>{'{data.name ?? \'Loading\'}'}</code> never shows <code>Loading</code>: the read
        throws before <code>??</code> is reached, and the compiler warns and names
        <code>ready()</code> (14.17). Ask the probe instead —
        <code>{'{data.ready() ? data.name : \'Loading\'}'}</code>.</p>

        <p>The sink is opened by <code>busy()</code> with nothing to serve, rather than by an absent
        value, and the difference shows on a derived value. Its body aborts on a source still in
        flight (2.12) and mints nothing (11.52), so it is absent for a moment that says nothing
        about the answer — and <code>busy()</code> is what says one is on its way (11.21).</p>

        <p><em>By default</em> is the whole of it: nothing in a template holds, and a hole that has
        not filled is the shape a page takes while it loads.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/put-a-value-in-the-markup')}>Expressions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A read before the value lands throws `NotLanded`">
        <Example
            id="not-landed-copy"
            title="`NotLanded` — a read before the value arrives"
            summary="A contact's email, with a Copy button. Open another contact and press Copy before the address lands."
            files={{
                'CopyEmail.abide': copyEmailSource,
            }}
        >
            <CopyEmail />
        </Example>

        <p>Outside the render flow there is no hole to leave, so the throw reaches your code. A
        click handler reading <code>contact.email</code> before the address lands gets
        <code>NotLanded</code>, and catches it by brand — a <code>NotLanded</code> is "not yet" and
        a <code>Failed</code> is "no" (2.14), so the <code>instanceof</code> arm never swallows a
        refusal. Anything else is rethrown.</p>

        <p><code>contact.busy()</code> is the probe the status line reads, and a probe never throws
        (3.1), which is why the line can say "loading…" in the same moment the read beside it cannot
        answer.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The first load and every one after are `ready()` apart">
        <Example
            id="probe-refresh"
            title="The first load and every one after are `ready()` apart"
            summary="An invoice with a Refresh beside it. Wait for the figures, then press Refresh and watch what does not disappear."
            files={{
                'RefreshInvoice.abide': refreshInvoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <RefreshInvoice />
        </Example>

        <p>Both states are busy. What separates them is whether there is anything on screen to keep:
        a first load is <code>busy() &amp;&amp; !ready()</code> and a reload is <code>busy()
        &amp;&amp; ready()</code>, because <strong><code>ready()</code> does not move for a load in
        flight</strong>. A spinner reads the conjunction; a table keeps rendering the rows it
        already has.</p>

        <p>One boolean cannot tell those apart, which is why the hand-written arm carries two: a
        first load has nothing to show and a reload has, so a single flag blanks the table every
        time somebody presses Refresh.</p>

        <p>An <code>invalidate()</code> is the other story: it says what is held is
        <strong>wrong</strong>, not merely stale, and it runs nothing and wakes nobody (7.3, 7.14).
        The next read or probe of the value is what runs the producer (3.2, 7.17), which stops
        serving what is held and loads with nothing underneath (7.8) — a first load again. That is
        the whole difference between the two triggers, and it is the one a reader can see.</p>

        <p><code>ready()</code> and <code>error()</code> are <strong>orthogonal, not
        opposite</strong>. One asks whether there is a value to show, the other whether the last
        write or production was refused, and a refused write over a landed value answers yes to both
        — which leaves a bound input on screen beside its own message. An accepted write clears the
        refusal.</p>

        <p>Reading a probe subscribes you to <strong>that probe alone</strong>. A value change does
        not wake a <code>busy()</code> reader, and a <code>busy()</code> flip does not wake a value
        reader, which is why the spinner and the table do not re-render each other.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A derived value is busy while what it reads is">
        <Example
            id="probe-derived"
            title="A derived value is busy while what it reads is"
            summary="A count of unpaid invoices, derived from a list still loading. Reload the page and watch the figure before the list lands."
            files={{
                'Unpaid.abide': unpaidSource,
            }}
        >
            <Unpaid />
        </Example>

        <p>A derived value answers <code>busy()</code> for <strong>its own load or any value its
        body read</strong> (11.21). Its body never runs over nothing: reading <code>rows</code>
        before the list lands throws and abandons the run (2.12), so the count is absent rather than
        a confident "0 unpaid". Absent says nothing about why, and the probes do —
        <code>busy()</code> says an answer is on its way, and <code>ready()</code> holds off until
        everything the body read has landed (11.58).</p>

        <p>It is the same <strong>any, not all</strong> question a selection asks, over the set the
        body already tracks in order to recompute. The answer is taken on the read that asks for it,
        walking those sources — no subscription per source, the list being the one tracking already
        keeps. Depth is the chain, not the whole graph.</p>

        <p>Deriving it at the recompute instead would miss the case it is most wanted for. A
        <code>refresh()</code> reloads a source while its value stands (7.4), so nothing recomputes,
        and a count taken at the last recompute reads false for the whole reload window. An
        <code>invalidate()</code> sends nothing (7.3), and the window opens on the read of the
        derived value, which runs the source's producer first (7.13).</p>

        <p><code>busy()</code> and <code>ready()</code> are the probes that propagate (11.20): a
        derived value is busy where anything it read is busy, and ready only where everything it
        read is (11.58). <code>error()</code> does not, because <code>Failures</code> does not — a
        read inside a body widens no union, so a propagated refusal would sit unnarrowable at every
        reader. Adoption is the other case: a body handing back a <code>Reactive</code> forwards
        every probe, <code>error()</code> included (11.50).</p>

        <p>Two ways to ask for the hole instead of the wait. Read the <strong>probe</strong> rather
        than the value — <code>rows.busy()</code> subscribes to that probe and is not a read of the
        value, so it never makes its own reader busy. Or <code>peek()</code>, which is already
        reading without joining the flow. A failure needs none of this: a source that failed with
        nothing landed throws on the read (2.2), so the body fails with it and the failure is the
        derived value's own. A <strong>keyed</strong> value propagates nothing, its body being
        untracked (11.23).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/derive-a-value-from-other-values')}>Derived values</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`error()` hands the failure back instead of throwing">
        <Example
            id="probe-error"
            title="`s.error()` hands the failure back rather than throwing"
            summary="An invoice whose billing service is down. The page renders the message instead of letting it reach the error page."
            files={{
                'UnreachableInvoice.abide': unreachableInvoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <UnreachableInvoice />
        </Example>

        <p>A first load that failed has no value, so the read throws and the failure reaches the
        nearest <code>{'{#try}'}</code> or <code>error.abide</code> without every call site
        checking. Escalating is the default, and this is how a page declines it: read the failure
        off the value and render a message instead. The retry clears it, which is the half a
        hand-kept third variable forgets.</p>

        <p><code>invoice</code> adopts the call, and every probe forwards through an adoption,
        <code>error()</code> included (11.50), so the handler's refusal is
        <code>invoice.error()</code>. The Retry reaches the load it adopted (11.51).</p>

        <p>A <strong>refused write</strong> never throws, whatever is standing: the gates refused
        something going in, and a read waits on a producer. A <strong>failed reload</strong> leaves
        the held value being served, so it does not throw either. Neither takes away what
        <code>ready()</code> answered, which is why escalation is bound to a producer that failed
        with nothing landed rather than to <code>error()</code> alone. The cost of that is a failed
        reload nothing escalates: it fills <code>error()</code>, warns on
        <code>abide:reactive</code>, and is otherwise invisible until something reads it.</p>

        <p><code>isError</code> narrows a caught failure by name, and <code>.data</code> is typed to
        what that failure declared.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Nothing in a template holds">
        <p>There is no form that waits. A read renders nothing and opens a sink the value fills
        later, and <code>{'{#if invoice.ready()}'}</code> gives you something to show in the
        meantime — those are the two, and neither of them stops the document.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{invoice}'}</code></td><td><b class="verdict-no">no</b><span class="verdict-why"> — the read renders nothing and opens a sink the value fills later</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#if invoice.ready()}'}</code></td><td><b class="verdict-no">no</b><span class="verdict-why"> — the <code>{'{:else}'}</code> renders while it loads</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await invoice</code> inside a handler</td><td><b class="verdict-yes">yes</b><span class="verdict-why"> — and that is the value being awaited, not a position</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The last one is the section below, and it is not a template form at all: a slot is
        compiled into an ordinary function, and so is component setup, so
        <code>{'{await invoice}'}</code> and a top-level <code>await</code> in a
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> are both refused. Waiting is something you do to a
        <strong>value</strong>, inside a function of your own; no place in the page does it to the
        page.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/show-markup-conditionally')}>Conditionals</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="On a server the value carries its own `await`">
        <p>It is the same <code>await</code> a handler writes, the value carrying the wait itself.
        <code>await invoice</code> resolves where <code>invoice.busy()</code> becomes false (2.7),
        and <code>then</code>, <code>catch</code> and <code>finally</code> reach that same promise
        by hand. Awaited mid-refresh it gets what the refresh lands, and awaited over a stream it
        gets the whole accumulation at close. Nothing about the read changes: <code>invoice()</code>
        still returns what has landed and never blocks. This is the only place waiting is spelled at
        all — there is no template form for it.</p>

        <p><code>catch</code> fires for a producer that failed with nothing landed, which is the one
        failure a read throws (2.8). A refused write and a failed reload both fill
        <code>error()</code>, and neither reaches <code>catch</code>.</p>

        <p><code>finally</code> is about the <strong>settling</strong>, not the value. A
        subscriber's copy of a channel settles at the first production to arrive and then keeps
        going (9.14, 2.7), so <code>finally</code> runs while it is still live.</p>

        <p>The value is a promise in the places JavaScript looks for one, so returning it from a
        handler hands back what it settled to. Nested in a returned object it is still a
        <code>Reactive</code>, which the compiler refuses — a member that would serialize to
        nothing.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The free probes answer over a selection">
        <p>The probes above are one value's. The free forms take a <strong>selection</strong> and
        answer over a set — one value, one keyed value, or every entry carrying a tag, or the whole
        scope when you pass nothing. Bare, that is anything in flight in this scope, which is what
        an app-wide progress strip wants.</p>

        <p>Only these two have a free form. <code>busy</code> asks whether ANY entry is in flight
        and <code>ready</code> whether EVERY entry has a value (13.2, 13.11), and an aggregate
        <code>error()</code> would have to pick a failure to hand back.</p>

        <p>A selection holds <strong>one signal</strong>, bumped when the count of matching
        in-flight entries crosses zero. Ten entries reloading together wake that strip twice, not
        twenty times.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> —
            <code>invalidate</code> against <code>refresh</code>, over one value or many</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a> — chunks, and
            when a stream reads as ready</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> — how
            the hole reaches the browser</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
